bugTracingExercise.prompt:
Below is a buggy attempt at detecting a cycle in a directed graph (the core of Course Schedule). It almost works — it passes on small acyclic examples — but fails on some cyclic graphs. Find the bug before running it.

```javascript
function canFinish(numCourses, prerequisites) {
  const graph = Array.from({ length: numCourses }, () => []);
  for (const [course, prereq] of prerequisites) {
    graph[course].push(prereq);
  }

  const visited = new Set();

  function hasCycle(course) {
    if (visited.has(course)) return true; // already seen this course somewhere
    visited.add(course);
    for (const prereq of graph[course]) {
      if (hasCycle(prereq)) return true;
    }
    return false;
  }

  for (let course = 0; course < numCourses; course++) {
    if (hasCycle(course)) return false;
  }
  return true;
}
```

What's wrong, and why does it pass on some inputs but not others? What's the minimal fix?

bugTracingExercise.solutionWalkthroughMarkdown:
**The bug:** `visited` only ever tracks "has this node been explored at any point, ever" — it never distinguishes between *(a)* a node that's part of the graph structure you're **currently in the middle of exploring** (i.e., on the current DFS recursion path) versus *(b)* a node you fully explored **earlier**, in a completely different branch, and which turned out to be cycle-free.

Concretely: imagine courses `0 -> 1`, `0 -> 2`, `1 -> 2` (no cycle — 2 has no prerequisites). Starting DFS from course `0`: visit `0`, recurse into `1`, from `1` recurse into `2` (mark all three visited, `2` has no further prereqs, returns false all the way up — correct, no cycle). Now the outer loop moves to `course = 1`: `hasCycle(1)` immediately sees `visited.has(1)` is true (from the earlier traversal) and returns `true` — **falsely reporting a cycle**, even though there isn't one. The bug isn't in graphs *with* cycles; it's a false-positive on cycle-free graphs where a node is legitimately revisited from a different starting point after being fully cleared.

(The same single `visited` set can also miss genuine cycles that only show up in certain traversal orders, for the same underlying reason: it can't tell 'currently mid-exploration' from 'fully explored and safe' apart.)

**The fix:** track two separate states — nodes currently *on the active recursion path* ("in progress") versus nodes that have been *fully explored and confirmed safe* ("done"). A cycle exists only if you reach a node that's currently "in progress" (i.e., an ancestor of itself on the current path) — reaching a "done" node is fine and should short-circuit to `false` immediately (no need to re-explore it).

```javascript
function canFinish(numCourses, prerequisites) {
  const graph = Array.from({ length: numCourses }, () => []);
  for (const [course, prereq] of prerequisites) {
    graph[course].push(prereq);
  }

  const inProgress = new Set();
  const done = new Set();

  function hasCycle(course) {
    if (done.has(course)) return false;   // fully explored earlier, confirmed safe
    if (inProgress.has(course)) return true; // revisited while still on the current path -> cycle

    inProgress.add(course);
    for (const prereq of graph[course]) {
      if (hasCycle(prereq)) return true;
    }
    inProgress.delete(course);
    done.add(course);
    return false;
  }

  for (let course = 0; course < numCourses; course++) {
    if (hasCycle(course)) return false;
  }
  return true;
}
```

This is exactly the "visited vs. currently-on-recursion-stack" distinction flagged in the Common Mistakes section above — internalizing this pattern (sometimes called the three-color/white-gray-black DFS marking scheme) is what separates a correct directed-cycle-detection implementation from one that only happens to work on the examples you tried.

bugTracingExercise.prompt:
Below is a buggy attempt at House Robber (given an array of house values, no two adjacent houses can be robbed, maximize total loot). It looks like a reasonable DP solution and passes on some inputs, but gives the wrong answer on others. Find the bug before running it.

```javascript
function rob(nums) {
  if (nums.length === 0) return 0;
  if (nums.length === 1) return nums[0];

  const dp = new Array(nums.length);
  dp[0] = nums[0];
  dp[1] = nums[1];

  for (let i = 2; i < nums.length; i++) {
    dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i - 1], dp[i - 2] + nums[i]);
  }

  return dp[nums.length - 1];
}
```

What's wrong, and on what kind of input does it fail? What's the minimal fix?

bugTracingExercise.solutionWalkthroughMarkdown:
**The bug is the base case `dp[1] = nums[1]`.** `dp[i]` is supposed to mean "the maximum loot achievable considering houses `0` through `i`" — not "the value of house `i` alone." For `dp[1]`, that means: the best of *either* robbing house 0, *or* robbing house 1 (you can't rob both if they're small, but more importantly, you're never forced to rob house 1 just because you're considering it — you're choosing the best option up to that point). The correct base case is `dp[1] = Math.max(nums[0], nums[1])`, not `dp[1] = nums[1]`.

**Why it passes on some inputs:** if `nums[1] >= nums[0]` (house 1 is worth at least as much as house 0), then `Math.max(nums[0], nums[1])` equals `nums[1]` anyway, so the bug produces the same result by coincidence. It only surfaces when `nums[0] > nums[1]` — e.g. `nums = [5, 1, 1, 5]`. Correct answer: rob houses 0 and 3 for `5 + 5 = 10`. But with the bug, `dp[1] = 1` (wrongly discarding the fact that house 0's value of 5 was available at this point), and that wrong `dp[1] = 1` then propagates forward: `dp[2] = max(dp[1], dp[0] + nums[2]) = max(1, 5 + 1) = 6`, `dp[3] = max(dp[2], dp[1] + nums[3]) = max(6, 1 + 5) = 6` — the algorithm returns `6` instead of the correct `10`, because the corrupted `dp[1]` silently undercounts the option of having robbed house 0 alone up through index 1.

**The fix:**
```javascript
function rob(nums) {
  if (nums.length === 0) return 0;
  if (nums.length === 1) return nums[0];

  const dp = new Array(nums.length);
  dp[0] = nums[0];
  dp[1] = Math.max(nums[0], nums[1]); // fixed: best of the two options up through index 1

  for (let i = 2; i < nums.length; i++) {
    dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i - 1], dp[i - 2] + nums[i]);
  }

  return dp[nums.length - 1];
}
```

The general lesson (see Common Mistakes above): **a base case has to satisfy the same definition of `dp[i]` as every other entry** — "the best answer considering everything up to and including i" — not just "whatever value happens to be sitting at that index in the input." Whenever a DP solution passes some tests and silently fails others with no crash, mismatched base-case semantics is one of the first things worth checking by hand-tracing the first 2-3 entries of the table."
